1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,080

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:09,880
So I'm excited to talk
today about our recent work

3
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:13,360
on using transformers for
reinforcement learning.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:17,860
And this is joint work with
a bunch of really exciting

5
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:21,220
collaborators, most of
them at UC Berkeley,

6
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:24,910
and some of them at
Facebook, and Google.

7
00:00:24,910 --> 00:00:27,970
I should mention, this
work was led by very two

8
00:00:27,970 --> 00:00:29,260
talented undergrads--

9
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:31,420
Lili Chen and Kevin Lu.

10
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:35,020
And I'm excited to present
the results we had.

11
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:40,280
So let's try to motivate why we
even care about this problem.

12
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:45,430
So we have seen in the
last three or four years

13
00:00:45,430 --> 00:00:49,450
that transformers, since
their introduction in 2017,

14
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:52,960
have taken over lots and
lots of different fields

15
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:55,550
of artificial intelligence.

16
00:00:55,550 --> 00:00:59,770
So we saw them having a big
impact for language processing.

17
00:00:59,770 --> 00:01:03,040
We saw them being used
for vision, in the vision

18
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:05,440
transformer very recently.

19
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:09,070
They were in nature trying
to solve protein folding.

20
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:12,550
And very soon they might just
replace us computer scientists

21
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:16,210
by having automatically
generate code.

22
00:01:16,210 --> 00:01:17,830
So with all of
these advances, it

23
00:01:17,830 --> 00:01:20,530
seems like we are
getting closer to having

24
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:24,370
a unified model
for decision making

25
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:26,320
for artificial intelligence.

26
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:29,290
But artificial
intelligence is much

27
00:01:29,290 --> 00:01:32,230
more about not just
having perception but also

28
00:01:32,230 --> 00:01:35,850
using the perception
knowledge to make decisions.

29
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:39,080
And this is what this
talk is going to be about.

30
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:41,110
But before I go into
actually thinking

31
00:01:41,110 --> 00:01:44,260
about how we will use these
models for decision making,

32
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:47,230
here is a motivation
for why I think it is

33
00:01:47,230 --> 00:01:49,750
important to ask this question.

34
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:54,130
So unlike models
for RL, when we look

35
00:01:54,130 --> 00:01:57,610
at transformers for
perception modalities

36
00:01:57,610 --> 00:01:59,800
like I showed in
the previous slide,

37
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:02,830
we find that these
models are very scalable

38
00:02:02,830 --> 00:02:06,490
and have very stable
training dynamics.

39
00:02:06,490 --> 00:02:10,120
So you can keep-- as long as
you have enough computation

40
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:13,070
and you have more and more
data that can be sourced.

41
00:02:13,070 --> 00:02:15,310
you can train bigger
and bigger models.

42
00:02:15,310 --> 00:02:19,930
And you'll see very smooth
reductions in the loss.

43
00:02:19,930 --> 00:02:24,250
And the overall training
dynamics are very, very stable.

44
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:28,000
And this makes it very easy for
practitioners and researchers

45
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,900
to build these models
and learn richer

46
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:34,190
and richer distributions.

47
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:36,760
So like I said, all
of these advances

48
00:02:36,760 --> 00:02:39,430
have so far occurred
in perception.

49
00:02:39,430 --> 00:02:41,170
What we're interested
in this talk

50
00:02:41,170 --> 00:02:45,010
is to think about how we can
go from perception, looking

51
00:02:45,010 --> 00:02:47,650
at images, looking at
text, and all these kinds

52
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:51,190
of sensory signals to then
going into the field of actually

53
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:55,330
taking actions and
making our agents

54
00:02:55,330 --> 00:02:56,815
do interesting
things in the world.

55
00:02:56,815 --> 00:02:59,380

56
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:01,840
And here, throughout
the talk, we

57
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:05,350
should be thinking about why
this perspective is going

58
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:08,860
to enable us to do scalable
learning, like I showed

59
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:11,530
in the previous slide,
otherwise bringing stability

60
00:03:11,530 --> 00:03:13,570
into the whole procedure.

61
00:03:13,570 --> 00:03:17,080
So sequential decision
making is a very broad area.

62
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:21,550
And what I'm specifically
going to be focusing on today,

63
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:24,070
is one route to sequential
decision making,

64
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:26,230
that's reinforcement learning.

65
00:03:26,230 --> 00:03:31,690
So just as a brief background,
what is reinforcement learning?

66
00:03:31,690 --> 00:03:36,640
So we are given an agent
who is in a current state.

67
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:40,060
And the agent is going to
interact with the environment

68
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:42,550
by taking actions.

69
00:03:42,550 --> 00:03:45,580
And by taking these
actions, the environment

70
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:49,570
is going to return to
it a reward for how good

71
00:03:49,570 --> 00:03:52,900
that action was, as well as
the next state into which

72
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:54,400
the agent will transition.

73
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:58,720
And this whole feedback
loop will continue on.

74
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:01,780
The goal here for
an intelligent agent

75
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:04,850
is to then, using
trial and error,

76
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:08,380
so try out different actions,
see what rewards it leads to,

77
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:12,520
learn a policy, which maps
your states to actions such

78
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:16,779
that the policy maximizes the
agent's cumulative rewards

79
00:04:16,779 --> 00:04:18,620
over time horizon.

80
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:20,110
So you take a
sequence of actions.

81
00:04:20,110 --> 00:04:22,330
And then based on the
reward you accumulate

82
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:24,790
for that sequence
of actions, we'll

83
00:04:24,790 --> 00:04:27,850
judge how good your policy is.

84
00:04:27,850 --> 00:04:31,990
This talk is also going
to be specifically focused

85
00:04:31,990 --> 00:04:33,940
on a form of
reinforcement learning

86
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:37,330
that goes by the name of
offline reinforcement learning.

87
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:40,870
So the idea here is
that what changes

88
00:04:40,870 --> 00:04:42,760
from the previous picture
where I was talking

89
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:46,030
about online learning, online
reinforcement learning is that

90
00:04:46,030 --> 00:04:49,420
here, now instead
of doing actively

91
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:51,850
interacting with
the environment,

92
00:04:51,850 --> 00:04:55,670
you have a collection of
log data of interactions.

93
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:58,780
So think about some robot
that's going out in the fields.

94
00:04:58,780 --> 00:05:01,180
And it collects a
bunch of sensory data.

95
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:02,740
And you have all logged it.

96
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:07,000
And using that log data, you
now want to train another agent,

97
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,720
it could be another
robot, to then learn

98
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:12,940
something interesting
about that environment

99
00:05:12,940 --> 00:05:16,280
just by looking at log data.

100
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:19,670
So there's no trial
and error component,

101
00:05:19,670 --> 00:05:25,040
which is currently one of the
extensions of this framework,

102
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:26,300
which would be very exciting.

103
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:29,320
So I'll talk about this towards
the end of the talk, why it's

104
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:33,100
exciting to think about how
we can extend this framework

105
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:35,110
to include an
exploration component

106
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:37,840
and have trial and error.

107
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:42,280
OK, so now to go more
concretely into what

108
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:44,540
the motivating challenge
of this talk was now

109
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:46,900
that we've introduced RL.

110
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:50,110
So let's look at
some statistics.

111
00:05:50,110 --> 00:05:55,030
So large language models, they
have billions of parameters.

112
00:05:55,030 --> 00:06:01,490
And they have roughly about
100 layers in transformer.

113
00:06:01,490 --> 00:06:05,500
They're very stable to train
using supervised learning style

114
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:08,440
losses, which are
the building blocks

115
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:10,930
of autoregressive
generation, for instance,

116
00:06:10,930 --> 00:06:15,350
or for masked language
modeling as input.

117
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:20,080
And this is like a field
that's growing every day.

118
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:22,540
And there is a
course at Stanford

119
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:24,460
that we're all taking
just because it has had

120
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:29,110
such a monumental impact on AI.

121
00:06:29,110 --> 00:06:34,410
RL policies on the other hand,
and I'm talking about deep RL.

122
00:06:34,410 --> 00:06:37,330
The maximum they would
extend to is maybe

123
00:06:37,330 --> 00:06:41,440
millions of parameters
or 20 layers.

124
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:44,980
And what's really
unnerving is that they're

125
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:46,090
very unstable to train.

126
00:06:46,090 --> 00:06:49,480
So the current algorithms
for reinforcement learning,

127
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:52,910
they build on mostly
dynamic programming,

128
00:06:52,910 --> 00:06:55,240
which involves following
an inner loop optimization

129
00:06:55,240 --> 00:06:57,070
problem that's very unstable.

130
00:06:57,070 --> 00:07:00,340
And it's very common
to see practitioners

131
00:07:00,340 --> 00:07:03,830
in RL looking at rewards
that look like this.

132
00:07:03,830 --> 00:07:06,040
So what I really
want you to see here

133
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:10,870
is the variance in the returns
that we tend to get in RL.

134
00:07:10,870 --> 00:07:13,990
It's really huge even
after doing multiple rounds

135
00:07:13,990 --> 00:07:16,450
of experimentation.

136
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:18,970
And that is really at
the core with the fact

137
00:07:18,970 --> 00:07:26,080
that our algorithms [INAUDIBLE]
need better improvements

138
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:30,640
so that the performance can
be stably achieved by agents

139
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:33,350
in complex environments.

140
00:07:33,350 --> 00:07:37,870
So what this work
is hoping to do

141
00:07:37,870 --> 00:07:40,960
is it's going to
introduce transformers.

142
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:44,050
And I'll first show,
in one slide, what

143
00:07:44,050 --> 00:07:45,550
exactly that model looks like.

144
00:07:45,550 --> 00:07:49,030
And we're going to go into
deeper details of each

145
00:07:49,030 --> 00:07:50,920
of the components.

146
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:54,230
I have a question.

147
00:07:54,230 --> 00:07:56,780
Yeah, can I ask a
question real quick?

148
00:07:56,780 --> 00:07:57,710
Yes.

149
00:07:57,710 --> 00:08:00,160
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

150
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:04,730
I'm curious to know what is
the cause for why RL typically

151
00:08:04,730 --> 00:08:09,840
has several orders of
magnitude fewer parameters?

152
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:10,960
That's a great question.

153
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:14,520
So typically, when you think
about reinforcement learning

154
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:18,460
algorithms, in deep
RL in particular,

155
00:08:18,460 --> 00:08:22,300
so the most common
algorithms, for example,

156
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:29,300
have different networks playing
different roles in the task.

157
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:31,530
So you'll have a
network, for instance,

158
00:08:31,530 --> 00:08:33,435
playing the role of an actor.

159
00:08:33,435 --> 00:08:35,309
So it's trying to
figure out a policy.

160
00:08:35,309 --> 00:08:37,320
And then there'll be a
different network that's

161
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:39,120
playing the role of the critic.

162
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:43,169
And these networks
are trained on data

163
00:08:43,169 --> 00:08:45,120
that's adaptively gathered.

164
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:49,020
So unlike perception, where
you will have a huge data

165
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:54,630
set of interactions on which
you can train your models,

166
00:08:54,630 --> 00:08:58,530
in this case, the architectures
and even the environments

167
00:08:58,530 --> 00:09:03,450
to some extent are very
simplistic because of the fact

168
00:09:03,450 --> 00:09:07,920
that we are trying to train very
small components, the functions

169
00:09:07,920 --> 00:09:10,650
that we are training and then
bringing them all together.

170
00:09:10,650 --> 00:09:16,350
And these functions are often
trained in not super complex

171
00:09:16,350 --> 00:09:17,590
environments.

172
00:09:17,590 --> 00:09:20,040
So it's a mix of
different issues.

173
00:09:20,040 --> 00:09:23,070
I wouldn't say it's purely
just got to do with the fact

174
00:09:23,070 --> 00:09:26,400
that the learning
objectives are at fault,

175
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:29,070
but it's a combination of
the environments we use,

176
00:09:29,070 --> 00:09:31,470
the combination of
the targets that each

177
00:09:31,470 --> 00:09:33,930
of the neural networks
are predicting,

178
00:09:33,930 --> 00:09:37,020
which leads to
networks which are

179
00:09:37,020 --> 00:09:40,050
much bigger than
what we currently

180
00:09:40,050 --> 00:09:41,760
see tending to all fit.

181
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:44,970
And that's why it's very
common to see neural networks

182
00:09:44,970 --> 00:09:48,240
with much fewer layers
being used in RL

183
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:50,010
as opposed to perception.

184
00:09:50,010 --> 00:09:52,940

185
00:09:52,940 --> 00:09:53,450
Thank you.

186
00:09:53,450 --> 00:09:56,020

187
00:09:56,020 --> 00:09:57,680
You want to ask a question?

188
00:09:57,680 --> 00:09:58,870
Yeah.

189
00:09:58,870 --> 00:09:59,875
Yeah, I was going to.

190
00:09:59,875 --> 00:10:06,310
Is there a reason why you chose
a offline RL versus online RL?

191
00:10:06,310 --> 00:10:07,700
That's another great question.

192
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:11,650
So the question is why offline
RL as opposed to online RL.

193
00:10:11,650 --> 00:10:14,680
And the plain reason
is because this

194
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:18,460
is the first work trying to
look at reinforcement learning.

195
00:10:18,460 --> 00:10:23,380
So offline RL avoids this
problem of exploration.

196
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:26,080
You are given a large
data set of interactions.

197
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:29,270
You're not allowed to further
interact with the environment.

198
00:10:29,270 --> 00:10:31,030
So just from this
data set, you are

199
00:10:31,030 --> 00:10:35,290
trying to unearth a policy of
what the optimal agent would

200
00:10:35,290 --> 00:10:36,500
look like.

201
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:38,080
So it would be--

202
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:40,960
Like if you do online RL
wouldn't that like just

203
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:44,850
give you this opportunity
of exploration, basically?

204
00:10:44,850 --> 00:10:46,360
It would, it would.

205
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:51,070
But what it would also do, which
is technically challenging here

206
00:10:51,070 --> 00:10:54,610
is that the exploration
would be harder to encode.

207
00:10:54,610 --> 00:10:56,290
So offline RL is a first step.

208
00:10:56,290 --> 00:11:00,490
There's no reason why we should
not study RL online or cannot

209
00:11:00,490 --> 00:11:01,460
be done.

210
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:03,700
It's just that it provides
a more contained state

211
00:11:03,700 --> 00:11:07,405
of where ideas from transformers
will directly extend.

212
00:11:07,405 --> 00:11:13,320

213
00:11:13,320 --> 00:11:14,970
OK, sounds good.

214
00:11:14,970 --> 00:11:16,200
So let's look at the model.

215
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:19,330
And it's really
simple on purpose.

216
00:11:19,330 --> 00:11:21,120
So what we're going
to do is we're

217
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:24,960
going to look at our offline
data, which is essentially

218
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:26,850
in the form of trajectories.

219
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:32,340
So offline data would look like
a sequence of states, actions,

220
00:11:32,340 --> 00:11:35,670
returns, over
multiple time steps.

221
00:11:35,670 --> 00:11:37,270
It's a sequence.

222
00:11:37,270 --> 00:11:40,920
So it's natural to think
of as directly feeding it

223
00:11:40,920 --> 00:11:43,890
as input to a transformer.

224
00:11:43,890 --> 00:11:46,950
In this case, we use
a causal transformer

225
00:11:46,950 --> 00:11:50,170
as it's common in GPT.

226
00:11:50,170 --> 00:11:51,480
So we go from left to right.

227
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:55,020
And because this data set comes
with the notion of time step

228
00:11:55,020 --> 00:11:58,290
causality, here it's
much more well-intended

229
00:11:58,290 --> 00:12:01,560
than the general meaning
that used for perception.

230
00:12:01,560 --> 00:12:03,930
This is really
causality, how it should

231
00:12:03,930 --> 00:12:07,020
be in the perspective of time.

232
00:12:07,020 --> 00:12:09,630
What we predict out
of this transformer

233
00:12:09,630 --> 00:12:14,160
are the actions
conditioned on everything

234
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:17,710
that comes before that
token in the sequence.

235
00:12:17,710 --> 00:12:21,330
So if you want to predict the
action at this t minus 1 step,

236
00:12:21,330 --> 00:12:26,640
we will use everything that
came at timestep t minus 2

237
00:12:26,640 --> 00:12:32,970
as well as the returns and
states at timestep t minus 1.

238
00:12:32,970 --> 00:12:39,180
OK, so I will go into the
details of how exactly

239
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:41,220
each of these are encoded.

240
00:12:41,220 --> 00:12:45,150
But essentially, this
is in a one liner.

241
00:12:45,150 --> 00:12:48,420
It's taking the trajectory
data from the offline data,

242
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:50,970
treating it as a
sequence of tokens,

243
00:12:50,970 --> 00:12:52,830
passing it through a
causal transformer,

244
00:12:52,830 --> 00:12:56,850
and getting a sequence
of actions as the output.

245
00:12:56,850 --> 00:13:01,140
OK, so how exactly do
we do the forward pass

246
00:13:01,140 --> 00:13:03,580
through the network?

247
00:13:03,580 --> 00:13:07,360
So one important
aspect of this work,

248
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:12,600
which is the usestate,
actions, and this quantity

249
00:13:12,600 --> 00:13:14,890
called returns-to-go.

250
00:13:14,890 --> 00:13:17,250
So these are not direct rewards.

251
00:13:17,250 --> 00:13:18,450
These are returns-to-go.

252
00:13:18,450 --> 00:13:22,850
And let's see what
they really mean.

253
00:13:22,850 --> 00:13:28,060
So this is a trajectory that
goes as input to the system.

254
00:13:28,060 --> 00:13:30,970
And the returns-to-go
are the sum

255
00:13:30,970 --> 00:13:35,470
of rewards starting from
the current time step

256
00:13:35,470 --> 00:13:38,090
until the end of the episode.

257
00:13:38,090 --> 00:13:42,700
So really what we want the
transformer is to get better

258
00:13:42,700 --> 00:13:46,000
at using a target
return, this is

259
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,580
how you should think
of returns-to-go,

260
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:52,820
as the input in deciding
what action to take.

261
00:13:52,820 --> 00:13:55,520
This perspective is going
to have multiple advantages.

262
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:58,250
It will allow us to actually
do much more than offline RL

263
00:13:58,250 --> 00:14:01,490
and generalizing to
different tasks by just

264
00:14:01,490 --> 00:14:04,310
changing the returns-to-go.

265
00:14:04,310 --> 00:14:06,150
And here it's very important.

266
00:14:06,150 --> 00:14:10,940
So at time step 1, we will just
have the overall sum of rewards

267
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:12,710
for the entire trajectory.

268
00:14:12,710 --> 00:14:15,650
At time step 2, we
subtract the reward

269
00:14:15,650 --> 00:14:17,840
we get by taking
the first action

270
00:14:17,840 --> 00:14:20,120
and then have the sum of
rewards for the remainder

271
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:22,950
of the trajectory.

272
00:14:22,950 --> 00:14:26,580
OK, so that's why we
call it returns-to-go,

273
00:14:26,580 --> 00:14:30,450
like how many more
rewards in accumulation

274
00:14:30,450 --> 00:14:36,360
you need to acquire to fulfill
your reward, your return goal

275
00:14:36,360 --> 00:14:39,120
that you set in the beginning.

276
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:40,530
What is the output?

277
00:14:40,530 --> 00:14:44,320
The output is the sequence
of predicted actions.

278
00:14:44,320 --> 00:14:47,740
So as I showed in
the previous slide,

279
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:50,670
we used a call to the
transformer so we'll predict,

280
00:14:50,670 --> 00:14:55,260
in sequence, the
desired actions.

281
00:14:55,260 --> 00:15:01,200
The attention which is
going to be computed

282
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:04,560
inside the transformer will take
in an important hyperparameter

283
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:07,320
K, which is the context length.

284
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:09,270
We see that in
perception as well here.

285
00:15:09,270 --> 00:15:11,160
And for the rest
of the talk, I'm

286
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:14,160
going to use the notation K
to denote how many tokens,

287
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:17,430
in the past, would
be attending over

288
00:15:17,430 --> 00:15:21,980
to predict the action
and the current timestep.

289
00:15:21,980 --> 00:15:28,040
OK, so again, digging a
little bit deeper into code,

290
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:29,870
there are some
subtle differences

291
00:15:29,870 --> 00:15:35,570
with how a decision
transformer operates as opposed

292
00:15:35,570 --> 00:15:37,880
to a normal transformer.

293
00:15:37,880 --> 00:15:41,240
The first is that here
the timestep notion

294
00:15:41,240 --> 00:15:47,030
is going to have much
bigger semantics that

295
00:15:47,030 --> 00:15:50,210
extends across three tokens.

296
00:15:50,210 --> 00:15:55,850
So in perception, you just think
about the timestep for word,

297
00:15:55,850 --> 00:15:59,660
for instance like in
NLP or patch for vision.

298
00:15:59,660 --> 00:16:02,870
And in this case, we
will have a timestep

299
00:16:02,870 --> 00:16:04,700
encapsulating three tokens.

300
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:05,870
One for the states.

301
00:16:05,870 --> 00:16:06,950
One for the actions.

302
00:16:06,950 --> 00:16:08,990
And one for the rewards.

303
00:16:08,990 --> 00:16:13,560
And then we'll embed
each of these tokens.

304
00:16:13,560 --> 00:16:15,380
And then add the position
embedding as it's

305
00:16:15,380 --> 00:16:17,450
coming in the transformer.

306
00:16:17,450 --> 00:16:22,850
And we feed those inputs
to the transformer.

307
00:16:22,850 --> 00:16:24,800
At the output, we
only care about one

308
00:16:24,800 --> 00:16:27,950
of these feed tokens
in this default setup.

309
00:16:27,950 --> 00:16:29,780
I will show
experiments where even

310
00:16:29,780 --> 00:16:33,110
the other tokens might be of
interest as target predictions.

311
00:16:33,110 --> 00:16:34,830
But for now, let's
keep it simple.

312
00:16:34,830 --> 00:16:37,040
We want to learn a policy,
a policy of just trying

313
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:38,640
to predict actions.

314
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:44,750
So when we try to decode, we'll
only be looking at the actions

315
00:16:44,750 --> 00:16:48,440
from the hidden representation
in the final layer.

316
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:50,180
OK, so this is the forward pass.

317
00:16:50,180 --> 00:16:51,680
Now what do we do
with this network?

318
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:53,090
We train it.

319
00:16:53,090 --> 00:16:55,993
How do we train it?

320
00:16:55,993 --> 00:16:57,410
Just a quick
question on semantics

321
00:16:57,410 --> 00:17:01,350
there, if you go back one
slide, the plus in this case,

322
00:17:01,350 --> 00:17:03,045
the syntax means
that you are actually

323
00:17:03,045 --> 00:17:05,420
adding the values elementwise
wise and not concatenating,

324
00:17:05,420 --> 00:17:07,243
is that right?

325
00:17:07,243 --> 00:17:07,910
That is correct.

326
00:17:07,910 --> 00:17:08,952
OK, just wanted to check.

327
00:17:08,952 --> 00:17:13,640

328
00:17:13,640 --> 00:17:16,069
OK, so what's the loss function?

329
00:17:16,069 --> 00:17:16,819
Hold up on that.

330
00:17:16,819 --> 00:17:21,119
I thought it was concatenating,
why are we just adding it?

331
00:17:21,119 --> 00:17:24,069
Sorry, can you go back?

332
00:17:24,069 --> 00:17:26,470
Yeah, I think it's
a design choice.

333
00:17:26,470 --> 00:17:27,890
You can concatenate.

334
00:17:27,890 --> 00:17:29,289
You can add it.

335
00:17:29,289 --> 00:17:31,630
It leads to different
functions being encoded.

336
00:17:31,630 --> 00:17:34,430
In our case, it is addition.

337
00:17:34,430 --> 00:17:34,930
OK.

338
00:17:34,930 --> 00:17:37,770

339
00:17:37,770 --> 00:17:40,290
Did you try the other one
and it just didn't work

340
00:17:40,290 --> 00:17:42,750
or why is that?

341
00:17:42,750 --> 00:17:44,970
Because I think,
intuitively, concatenating

342
00:17:44,970 --> 00:17:48,860
would make more sense.

343
00:17:48,860 --> 00:17:53,920
So I think both of them
have different use cases

344
00:17:53,920 --> 00:17:55,120
for the functional encoding.

345
00:17:55,120 --> 00:18:00,290
Like one is really mixing in
the embeddings for the state

346
00:18:00,290 --> 00:18:01,720
and basically shifting it.

347
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:05,020
So when you add
something, if you

348
00:18:05,020 --> 00:18:09,250
think of the embedding
of the states as a vector

349
00:18:09,250 --> 00:18:13,180
and you add something, you
are actually shifting it.

350
00:18:13,180 --> 00:18:15,010
Whereas in the
concatenation case,

351
00:18:15,010 --> 00:18:17,755
you are actually increasing the
dimensionality of this space.

352
00:18:17,755 --> 00:18:20,460

353
00:18:20,460 --> 00:18:23,460
So those are different
choices which are

354
00:18:23,460 --> 00:18:25,660
doing very different things.

355
00:18:25,660 --> 00:18:28,240
We found this one to be better.

356
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:32,190
I'm not sure I remember if the
results was very significantly

357
00:18:32,190 --> 00:18:33,930
different if you would
concatenate them,

358
00:18:33,930 --> 00:18:37,440
but this is the one
which we operate with.

359
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:39,620
But wouldn't there-- because
if you're shifting it,

360
00:18:39,620 --> 00:18:41,370
like if you have an
embedding for a state,

361
00:18:41,370 --> 00:18:43,710
and let's say you
perform certain actions,

362
00:18:43,710 --> 00:18:46,620
and you like end up at
the same state again,

363
00:18:46,620 --> 00:18:49,587
you would want these
embeddings to be the same.

364
00:18:49,587 --> 00:18:51,420
However, now you're at
a different timestep,

365
00:18:51,420 --> 00:18:52,860
so like you shifted it.

366
00:18:52,860 --> 00:18:56,340
So wouldn't that be
like harder to learn?

367
00:18:56,340 --> 00:18:59,860
So there's a bigger and
interesting question in that.

368
00:18:59,860 --> 00:19:04,540
What you said is basically are
we losing the Markov property?

369
00:19:04,540 --> 00:19:08,620
Because as you said that if
you come back to the same state

370
00:19:08,620 --> 00:19:11,590
at a different
timestep, shouldn't we

371
00:19:11,590 --> 00:19:15,370
be doing similar operations?

372
00:19:15,370 --> 00:19:17,470
And the answer here is, yes.

373
00:19:17,470 --> 00:19:19,900
We are actually
being non-Markov.

374
00:19:19,900 --> 00:19:22,660
And this might seem very
non-intuitive at first

375
00:19:22,660 --> 00:19:29,530
that why is non-Markovness
important here?

376
00:19:29,530 --> 00:19:33,340
And I want to refer
to another paper which

377
00:19:33,340 --> 00:19:36,440
came very much in conjunction
with this type of transformer

378
00:19:36,440 --> 00:19:38,470
that actually shows
in more detail.

379
00:19:38,470 --> 00:19:40,180
And it's basically
saying that if you

380
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:43,090
were trying to predict
the transition dynamics,

381
00:19:43,090 --> 00:19:46,570
then you could have actually
had a Markovian system built

382
00:19:46,570 --> 00:19:50,660
in here, which would
do just as good.

383
00:19:50,660 --> 00:19:55,390
However, from the perspective
of trying to actually predict

384
00:19:55,390 --> 00:20:00,730
actions, it does help to look
at the previous timesteps,

385
00:20:00,730 --> 00:20:03,160
even more so when you
have missing observations.

386
00:20:03,160 --> 00:20:07,240
So for instance, if you
have the observations being

387
00:20:07,240 --> 00:20:11,920
a subset of the true state, so
looking at the previous states

388
00:20:11,920 --> 00:20:16,330
and actions helps you
better fill in the missing

389
00:20:16,330 --> 00:20:17,390
pieces in some sense.

390
00:20:17,390 --> 00:20:21,010
So this is commonly known as
partial observability, where

391
00:20:21,010 --> 00:20:22,540
by looking at the
previous tokens,

392
00:20:22,540 --> 00:20:25,840
you can do a better
job at predicting

393
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:31,230
the actions you should take
at the current timestep.

394
00:20:31,230 --> 00:20:35,040
So non-Markovness is on purpose.

395
00:20:35,040 --> 00:20:37,920
And it's not
intuitive, but I think

396
00:20:37,920 --> 00:20:41,760
it's one of the things that
separates this framework

397
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:44,650
from existing ones.

398
00:20:44,650 --> 00:20:46,290
So it will basically help you--

399
00:20:46,290 --> 00:20:51,000
because like RL usually works
better on infinite horizon

400
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:51,810
problems, right?

401
00:20:51,810 --> 00:20:53,610
So technically, the
way you formulate it,

402
00:20:53,610 --> 00:20:56,830
it would work better on finite
horizon problems, I'm assuming.

403
00:20:56,830 --> 00:20:58,830
Because you want to take
different actions based

404
00:20:58,830 --> 00:21:00,840
on a history, based
on given the fact

405
00:21:00,840 --> 00:21:03,850
that now you would look
at a different time step.

406
00:21:03,850 --> 00:21:04,350
Yeah.

407
00:21:04,350 --> 00:21:06,960
Yeah, so if you wanted to
work on infinite horizon,

408
00:21:06,960 --> 00:21:10,740
maybe something like this
would work just as well

409
00:21:10,740 --> 00:21:12,540
to get that effect.

410
00:21:12,540 --> 00:21:16,920
In this case, we were using
a discount factor of 1

411
00:21:16,920 --> 00:21:19,710
or basically like no
discounting at all.

412
00:21:19,710 --> 00:21:21,600
But you're right,
if I think we really

413
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:23,610
want to extend it
to infinite horizon,

414
00:21:23,610 --> 00:21:26,650
we would need to change
the discount factor.

415
00:21:26,650 --> 00:21:27,150
OK, thanks.

