457
00:23:13,030 --> 00:23:14,230
So let's go ahead.

458
00:23:14,230 --> 00:23:16,960
So we have defined our
model which is going

459
00:23:16,960 --> 00:23:21,040
to look at these trajectories.

460
00:23:21,040 --> 00:23:23,500
And now, let's see
how we train it.

461
00:23:23,500 --> 00:23:25,960
So very simple.

462
00:23:25,960 --> 00:23:28,000
We are trying to
predict actions.

463
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,700
We'll try to match them to the
ones we have in our data set.

464
00:23:30,700 --> 00:23:33,430
If they are continuous,
using the mean squared error.

465
00:23:33,430 --> 00:23:38,120
If they are discrete then
we can use a cross-entropy.

466
00:23:38,120 --> 00:23:43,400
So but there is something very
deep in here for RL research,

467
00:23:43,400 --> 00:23:47,350
which is that these objectives
are very stable to train

468
00:23:47,350 --> 00:23:49,720
and easy to regularize
because they've been developed

469
00:23:49,720 --> 00:23:52,260
for supervised learning.

470
00:23:52,260 --> 00:23:54,480
In contrast, what
RL is more used to

471
00:23:54,480 --> 00:23:57,120
is dynamic programming
style objectives,

472
00:23:57,120 --> 00:23:59,520
which are based on
the Bellman equation.

473
00:23:59,520 --> 00:24:04,990
And those end up being much
harder to optimize and scale.

474
00:24:04,990 --> 00:24:08,250
And that's why you see a lot
of the variance in the results

475
00:24:08,250 --> 00:24:11,150
as well.

476
00:24:11,150 --> 00:24:13,320
OK, so this is how
we train the model.

477
00:24:13,320 --> 00:24:14,720
Now, how do we use the model?

478
00:24:14,720 --> 00:24:17,360
And that's the
point about trying

479
00:24:17,360 --> 00:24:19,800
to do rollout for the model.

480
00:24:19,800 --> 00:24:23,750
So here again, this is
going to be similar to doing

481
00:24:23,750 --> 00:24:26,390
an autoregressive generation.

482
00:24:26,390 --> 00:24:28,290
There is an
important token here,

483
00:24:28,290 --> 00:24:30,350
which was the returns-to-go.

484
00:24:30,350 --> 00:24:33,740
And what we need to
set during evaluation,

485
00:24:33,740 --> 00:24:38,060
presumably, we want
expert level performance

486
00:24:38,060 --> 00:24:41,030
because that will have
the highest returns.

487
00:24:41,030 --> 00:24:45,170
So we set the initial
returns-to-go not

488
00:24:45,170 --> 00:24:47,060
based on that trajectory,
because now we

489
00:24:47,060 --> 00:24:48,060
don't have a trajectory.

490
00:24:48,060 --> 00:24:49,440
We're in a generator trajectory.

491
00:24:49,440 --> 00:24:50,960
So this is at inference time.

492
00:24:50,960 --> 00:24:54,660
So we will set it to the
expert return for instance.

493
00:24:54,660 --> 00:24:56,960
So in code, what this
whole procedure would

494
00:24:56,960 --> 00:25:00,440
look like is basically you
set this returns-to-go token

495
00:25:00,440 --> 00:25:03,410
to some target return.

496
00:25:03,410 --> 00:25:08,600
And you set your
initial state to one

497
00:25:08,600 --> 00:25:12,080
from the environment
distribution of initial states.

498
00:25:12,080 --> 00:25:15,890
And then you just roll out
your decision transformer.

499
00:25:15,890 --> 00:25:18,740
So you get a new action.

500
00:25:18,740 --> 00:25:20,750
And this action will
also give you a state

501
00:25:20,750 --> 00:25:23,300
and reward from the environment.

502
00:25:23,300 --> 00:25:26,090
You append them
to your sequence.

503
00:25:26,090 --> 00:25:29,210
And you get a new returns-to-go.

504
00:25:29,210 --> 00:25:31,718
And you take just
the context in K

505
00:25:31,718 --> 00:25:34,010
because that's what's used
by the transformer to making

506
00:25:34,010 --> 00:25:36,110
predictions and
then feed it back

507
00:25:36,110 --> 00:25:38,330
to the decision transformer.

508
00:25:38,330 --> 00:25:41,900
So it's regular
autoregressive generation,

509
00:25:41,900 --> 00:25:45,740
but the only key point to
notice is how you initialize

510
00:25:45,740 --> 00:25:50,230
the transformer for RL.

