877
00:44:54,550 --> 00:44:57,550
But yeah that's a
reasonable estimate.

878
00:44:57,550 --> 00:44:58,240
Yeah, got it.

879
00:44:58,240 --> 00:44:58,740
Thanks.

880
00:44:58,740 --> 00:45:03,720

881
00:45:03,720 --> 00:45:08,490
OK, so actually while
doing this project,

882
00:45:08,490 --> 00:45:11,100
we thought of a
baseline which we

883
00:45:11,100 --> 00:45:13,710
were surprised is not there
in previous literature

884
00:45:13,710 --> 00:45:16,710
on offline RL but
makes very much sense.

885
00:45:16,710 --> 00:45:18,825
And we thought we
should also think about

886
00:45:18,825 --> 00:45:20,700
whether decision
transformer they're actually

887
00:45:20,700 --> 00:45:23,160
doing something very
similar to that baseline.

888
00:45:23,160 --> 00:45:27,023
And the baseline is what we call
as percent behavioral cloning.

889
00:45:27,023 --> 00:45:28,440
So behavioral
cloning what it does

890
00:45:28,440 --> 00:45:31,320
is basically it
ignores the returns

891
00:45:31,320 --> 00:45:39,270
and simply imitates the agent by
just trying to map the actions

892
00:45:39,270 --> 00:45:42,280
given the constraints.

893
00:45:42,280 --> 00:45:45,865
This is not a good idea
with an offline data

894
00:45:45,865 --> 00:45:48,700
set which will have
trajectories of both low returns

895
00:45:48,700 --> 00:45:50,870
and high returns.

896
00:45:50,870 --> 00:45:52,900
So traditional
behaviour cloning,

897
00:45:52,900 --> 00:45:56,695
it's common to see that as a
baseline in offline RL methods.

898
00:45:56,695 --> 00:45:59,470

899
00:45:59,470 --> 00:46:02,810
And unless you have a very
high quality data set,

900
00:46:02,810 --> 00:46:05,840
it is not a good
baseline for offline RL.

901
00:46:05,840 --> 00:46:07,990
However, there is
a version that we

902
00:46:07,990 --> 00:46:10,000
call as percent
BC, which actually

903
00:46:10,000 --> 00:46:11,510
makes quite a lot of sense.

904
00:46:11,510 --> 00:46:16,000
And in this version, we filter
out the top trajectories

905
00:46:16,000 --> 00:46:18,990
from our offline dataset
and stop the ones

906
00:46:18,990 --> 00:46:20,240
that have the highest rewards.

907
00:46:20,240 --> 00:46:23,140
You know the rewards
for each transition.

908
00:46:23,140 --> 00:46:25,180
You calculate the returns
on the trajectories.

909
00:46:25,180 --> 00:46:29,170
And we take the trajectories
with the highest returns

910
00:46:29,170 --> 00:46:31,460
and keep a certain
percentage of them,

911
00:46:31,460 --> 00:46:35,450
which is going to be
hyperparameter here.

912
00:46:35,450 --> 00:46:39,520
And once you keep those top
fraction of your trajectories,

913
00:46:39,520 --> 00:46:43,330
you then just ask your
model to imitate them.

914
00:46:43,330 --> 00:46:46,290

915
00:46:46,290 --> 00:46:49,557
So imitation learning
also uses, especially when

916
00:46:49,557 --> 00:46:51,390
it's used in the form
of behavioral cloning,

917
00:46:51,390 --> 00:46:53,902
it uses supervised
learning essentially,

918
00:46:53,902 --> 00:46:55,360
it's a supervised
learning problem.

919
00:46:55,360 --> 00:46:57,150
So you could actually
also get supervised

920
00:46:57,150 --> 00:47:01,560
learning objective functions
if you did this filtering step.

921
00:47:01,560 --> 00:47:05,040

922
00:47:05,040 --> 00:47:06,720
And what do you find?

923
00:47:06,720 --> 00:47:10,013
Actually that for the moderate
and high data regimes,

924
00:47:10,013 --> 00:47:11,430
decision transformers
are actually

925
00:47:11,430 --> 00:47:13,210
very comparable to percent BC.

926
00:47:13,210 --> 00:47:15,030
So it's a very strong
baseline, which

927
00:47:15,030 --> 00:47:18,540
I think all of future work
in offline RL should include.

928
00:47:18,540 --> 00:47:21,390
There's actually
an ICLR submission

929
00:47:21,390 --> 00:47:25,410
from last week, which has a much
more detailed analysis on just

930
00:47:25,410 --> 00:47:28,570
this baseline that we
introduced in this paper.

931
00:47:28,570 --> 00:47:32,430
And what we do find is
that for low data regimes,

932
00:47:32,430 --> 00:47:35,010
the decision transformer
does much better

933
00:47:35,010 --> 00:47:36,300
than percent behavior cloning.

934
00:47:36,300 --> 00:47:38,070
So this is for the
Atari benchmarks

935
00:47:38,070 --> 00:47:40,660
where, like I
previously mentioned,

936
00:47:40,660 --> 00:47:45,810
we have a much smaller data
set as compared to the MuJoCo

937
00:47:45,810 --> 00:47:46,680
environments.

938
00:47:46,680 --> 00:47:51,600
And here we find that even after
varying the different fraction

939
00:47:51,600 --> 00:47:54,000
of the percentage
hyperparameter here,

940
00:47:54,000 --> 00:47:56,970
we are generally not able to
get the strong performance

941
00:47:56,970 --> 00:47:59,550
the decision transformer gets.

942
00:47:59,550 --> 00:48:04,770
So 10% BC basically means that
we filter and keep the top 10%

943
00:48:04,770 --> 00:48:06,510
of the trajectories.

944
00:48:06,510 --> 00:48:09,270
If you go even lower,
then the dataset

945
00:48:09,270 --> 00:48:13,050
becomes really small so the
baseline becomes meaningless.

946
00:48:13,050 --> 00:48:15,720
But for even though
reasonable ranges,

947
00:48:15,720 --> 00:48:17,520
we never find the
performance matching

948
00:48:17,520 --> 00:48:23,500
that of decision transformers
for these Atari benchmarks.

949
00:48:23,500 --> 00:48:26,920
Aditya, if I may.

950
00:48:26,920 --> 00:48:28,760
So notice in table
3, for example,

951
00:48:28,760 --> 00:48:30,427
which is not this
table but the one just

952
00:48:30,427 --> 00:48:31,440
before it in the paper.

953
00:48:31,440 --> 00:48:34,810
There's a report on the CQL
performance, which to me also

954
00:48:34,810 --> 00:48:39,610
feels intuitively pretty
similar to the percent BC,

955
00:48:39,610 --> 00:48:41,162
in the sense of you
pick trajectories

956
00:48:41,162 --> 00:48:43,420
you know are performing
well and you try and stay

957
00:48:43,420 --> 00:48:45,310
roughly within sort of
the same kind of kind

958
00:48:45,310 --> 00:48:51,880
of policy distribution and
state space distribution.

959
00:48:51,880 --> 00:48:53,150
I was curious on this one.

960
00:48:53,150 --> 00:48:55,300
Do you have a sense of what
the CQL performance was

961
00:48:55,300 --> 00:49:00,640
relative to, say, the
percent BC performance here?

962
00:49:00,640 --> 00:49:01,790
So that's a great question.

963
00:49:01,790 --> 00:49:06,410
So the question is
that even for CQL you

964
00:49:06,410 --> 00:49:09,380
rely on this notion
of pessimism where

965
00:49:09,380 --> 00:49:13,340
you want to pick trajectories
where you're more confident in.

966
00:49:13,340 --> 00:49:17,310
And try to make sure policy
remains in that region.

967
00:49:17,310 --> 00:49:21,420
So I don't have the numbers
of CQL on this table.

968
00:49:21,420 --> 00:49:26,090
But if you look at the
detailed results for Atari,

969
00:49:26,090 --> 00:49:31,790
then I think they should
have the CQL for sure,

970
00:49:31,790 --> 00:49:36,500
because that's the numbers
we are reporting here.

971
00:49:36,500 --> 00:49:39,560
So I can tell you what the
CQL performance is actually

972
00:49:39,560 --> 00:49:43,440
pretty good and it's very
competitive with the decision

973
00:49:43,440 --> 00:49:46,690
transformer for Atari.

974
00:49:46,690 --> 00:49:51,370
So this TD learning
baseline here is CQL.

975
00:49:51,370 --> 00:49:53,500
So by naturally by
extension, I would

976
00:49:53,500 --> 00:49:56,890
imagine it's doing
better than percent BC.

977
00:49:56,890 --> 00:49:59,615
Yeah, and I apologize
if this was mentioned

978
00:49:59,615 --> 00:50:00,490
and I just missed it.

979
00:50:00,490 --> 00:50:02,560
But do you have
the sense that this

980
00:50:02,560 --> 00:50:04,330
is basically like
a failure of CQL

981
00:50:04,330 --> 00:50:08,350
to be able to extrapolate well
or sort of stitch together

982
00:50:08,350 --> 00:50:11,432
different parts of trajectories,
whereas the decision

983
00:50:11,432 --> 00:50:13,390
transformer can sort of
make that extrapolation

984
00:50:13,390 --> 00:50:15,207
between-- you have
the first half of one

985
00:50:15,207 --> 00:50:16,290
trajectory is really good.

986
00:50:16,290 --> 00:50:17,663
The second half of one
trajectory is really good.

987
00:50:17,663 --> 00:50:18,990
And so you can actually
piece those together

988
00:50:18,990 --> 00:50:20,650
with the decision transform,
where you can't necessarily

989
00:50:20,650 --> 00:50:23,380
do that with CQL because
the path connecting those

990
00:50:23,380 --> 00:50:28,060
may not necessarily be well
covered by the behavior policy.

991
00:50:28,060 --> 00:50:29,830
Yeah, yeah.

992
00:50:29,830 --> 00:50:35,650
And this actually goes to one of
the intuitions which I did not

993
00:50:35,650 --> 00:50:38,410
emphasize too much, but we have
a discussion on that paper,

994
00:50:38,410 --> 00:50:41,560
where essentially
why do we expect

995
00:50:41,560 --> 00:50:44,050
a transformer or any
model for that matter

996
00:50:44,050 --> 00:50:47,110
to look at offline
data that's suboptimal

997
00:50:47,110 --> 00:50:49,450
and get something
in a policy that

998
00:50:49,450 --> 00:50:51,400
generates optimal rollouts?

999
00:50:51,400 --> 00:50:55,420
The intuition is that,
as Scott was mentioning,

1000
00:50:55,420 --> 00:51:01,330
you could perhaps stitch
together good behaviors

1001
00:51:01,330 --> 00:51:03,010
from suboptimal trajectories.

1002
00:51:03,010 --> 00:51:05,020
And that stitching
could perhaps lead

1003
00:51:05,020 --> 00:51:07,450
to a behavior that was
better than anything

1004
00:51:07,450 --> 00:51:11,470
you saw in individual
trajectories in a data set.

1005
00:51:11,470 --> 00:51:17,500
It's something we find early
evidence of in small scale

1006
00:51:17,500 --> 00:51:20,320
experiments for graphs.

1007
00:51:20,320 --> 00:51:23,560
And that's really our
hope also that something

1008
00:51:23,560 --> 00:51:26,470
that the transformer
is very good at,

1009
00:51:26,470 --> 00:51:29,060
because it can attend
to very long sequences.

1010
00:51:29,060 --> 00:51:33,190
So it could identify
those segments of behavior

1011
00:51:33,190 --> 00:51:35,890
which when stitched
together would give you

1012
00:51:35,890 --> 00:51:39,055
optimal behavior.

1013
00:51:39,055 --> 00:51:44,080

1014
00:51:44,080 --> 00:51:46,210
And it's very much possible.

1015
00:51:46,210 --> 00:51:48,850
And that is something unique
to decision transformers.

1016
00:51:48,850 --> 00:51:53,410
And something like CQL would
not be able to do percent BC

1017
00:51:53,410 --> 00:51:56,830
because it's filtering out
the data is automatically

1018
00:51:56,830 --> 00:51:58,750
being limited and not
being able to do that,

1019
00:51:58,750 --> 00:52:01,390
because those segments
of good behavior

1020
00:52:01,390 --> 00:52:03,490
could be in trajectories
which overall do not

1021
00:52:03,490 --> 00:52:05,120
have a high return.

1022
00:52:05,120 --> 00:52:07,750
So if you filter them
out, you are losing out

1023
00:52:07,750 --> 00:52:08,770
on that information.

1024
00:52:08,770 --> 00:52:13,560

1025
00:52:13,560 --> 00:52:17,190
OK, so I said there is a
hyperparameter, the context

1026
00:52:17,190 --> 00:52:21,370
in K. And like with
most of perception,

1027
00:52:21,370 --> 00:52:24,540
one of the big advantages
of transformers as opposed

1028
00:52:24,540 --> 00:52:26,940
to other sequence
models like LSTMs

1029
00:52:26,940 --> 00:52:31,230
is that they can process
fairly large sequences.

1030
00:52:31,230 --> 00:52:36,720
And here at first glance, it
might seem that being Markovian

1031
00:52:36,720 --> 00:52:40,710
would have been helpful for RL,
which also was a question that

1032
00:52:40,710 --> 00:52:42,390
was raised earlier.

1033
00:52:42,390 --> 00:52:45,060
So we did this experiment where
we did compare performance

1034
00:52:45,060 --> 00:52:48,060
with context in K plus 1.

1035
00:52:48,060 --> 00:52:53,190
And here, we had context
length between 30

1036
00:52:53,190 --> 00:52:56,040
for the environments
and 50 for Pong.

1037
00:52:56,040 --> 00:52:59,010
And we find that increasing
the context length

1038
00:52:59,010 --> 00:53:04,080
is very, very important
to get good performance.

1039
00:53:04,080 --> 00:53:14,170

1040
00:53:14,170 --> 00:53:19,000
OK, now so far I've showed
you how decision transformer,

1041
00:53:19,000 --> 00:53:21,620
which is very simple.

1042
00:53:21,620 --> 00:53:23,230
There was no slide
I had, which was

1043
00:53:23,230 --> 00:53:26,600
going into the details of
dynamic programming, which

1044
00:53:26,600 --> 00:53:29,080
is the crux of most RL.

1045
00:53:29,080 --> 00:53:31,360
This was just pure
supervised learning

1046
00:53:31,360 --> 00:53:34,000
in an autoregressive
framework that was getting

1047
00:53:34,000 --> 00:53:35,350
us this good performance.

1048
00:53:35,350 --> 00:53:38,160

1049
00:53:38,160 --> 00:53:42,200
What about cases where
this approach actually

1050
00:53:42,200 --> 00:53:47,090
starts outperforming some of
the traditional methods RL?

1051
00:53:47,090 --> 00:53:48,830
So to probe a
little bit further,

1052
00:53:48,830 --> 00:53:51,290
we started looking at
sparse reward environments.

1053
00:53:51,290 --> 00:53:56,210
And basically, we just took our
existing MuJoCo environments.

1054
00:53:56,210 --> 00:54:00,740
And then instead of giving
it the information for reward

1055
00:54:00,740 --> 00:54:03,050
for every transition,
we Fed it the

1056
00:54:03,050 --> 00:54:05,730
cumulative reward at the
end of the trajectory.

1057
00:54:05,730 --> 00:54:08,600
So every transition will have
zero reward except the very end

1058
00:54:08,600 --> 00:54:10,640
where you get the
entire reward at once.

1059
00:54:10,640 --> 00:54:16,170
It's a very sparse reward
scenario for that reason.

1060
00:54:16,170 --> 00:54:22,310
And here we find that, compared
to the original dense results,

1061
00:54:22,310 --> 00:54:27,770
the delayed results for DT will
deteriorate a little bit, which

1062
00:54:27,770 --> 00:54:31,250
was expected because
now you are withholding

1063
00:54:31,250 --> 00:54:33,950
some of the more fine grained
information at every time step.

1064
00:54:33,950 --> 00:54:38,420
But the drop is not too
significant compared

1065
00:54:38,420 --> 00:54:41,330
to the original DT
performance here.

1066
00:54:41,330 --> 00:54:43,280
Whereas for something
like CQL, there

1067
00:54:43,280 --> 00:54:45,840
is a drastic drop
in performance.

1068
00:54:45,840 --> 00:54:51,450
So CQL suffers quite a lot
in sparse reward scenarios,

1069
00:54:51,450 --> 00:54:54,220
but decision
transformer does not.

1070
00:54:54,220 --> 00:54:55,823
And just for
completeness, we also

1071
00:54:55,823 --> 00:54:57,990
have performance of the
behavior cloning and percent

1072
00:54:57,990 --> 00:55:00,280
behavior cloning
because they don't

1073
00:55:00,280 --> 00:55:03,820
look at reward information,
except maybe percent BC

1074
00:55:03,820 --> 00:55:06,880
looks at only for
pre-processing the data set.

1075
00:55:06,880 --> 00:55:09,790
These are agnostic to
whether the environments

1076
00:55:09,790 --> 00:55:11,200
have sparse rewards or not.

1077
00:55:11,200 --> 00:55:14,310

1078
00:55:14,310 --> 00:55:16,290
Would you expect
this to be different

1079
00:55:16,290 --> 00:55:18,520
if you were doing online RL?

1080
00:55:18,520 --> 00:55:24,480

1081
00:55:24,480 --> 00:55:29,280
What's the intuition
for it being different?

1082
00:55:29,280 --> 00:55:31,320
A priori, I would
say no, but maybe I'm

1083
00:55:31,320 --> 00:55:33,915
missing out on a key piece of
intuition behind that question.

1084
00:55:33,915 --> 00:55:37,820

1085
00:55:37,820 --> 00:55:42,800
I think that because you're
training like offline,

1086
00:55:42,800 --> 00:55:46,310
the next input will always
be the correct action

1087
00:55:46,310 --> 00:55:47,580
in that sense.

1088
00:55:47,580 --> 00:55:50,540
So you don't just deviate
and let go off the rails

1089
00:55:50,540 --> 00:55:52,650
technically, because
you just don't know.

1090
00:55:52,650 --> 00:55:57,980
So I could see how online would
have a really hard, cold start

1091
00:55:57,980 --> 00:55:59,750
basically because it
just doesn't know.

1092
00:55:59,750 --> 00:56:01,130
And it's just
tapping in the dark

1093
00:56:01,130 --> 00:56:06,170
until it maybe eventually
hits the jackpot.

1094
00:56:06,170 --> 00:56:09,110
Right, right, I think I
agree, that's a good piece

1095
00:56:09,110 --> 00:56:12,170
of intuition out there.

1096
00:56:12,170 --> 00:56:15,660

1097
00:56:15,660 --> 00:56:18,230
I think here that offline
RL is really getting rid

1098
00:56:18,230 --> 00:56:20,870
of the trial and
error aspect of it

1099
00:56:20,870 --> 00:56:24,260
and for sparse reward
environments that

1100
00:56:24,260 --> 00:56:26,170
would be harder.

1101
00:56:26,170 --> 00:56:34,730
So the drop in DT performance
should be more prominent there.

1102
00:56:34,730 --> 00:56:36,320
I'm not sure how
it would compare

1103
00:56:36,320 --> 00:56:41,990
with the job performance
for other algorithms.

1104
00:56:41,990 --> 00:56:46,880
But it does seem like
an interesting setup

1105
00:56:46,880 --> 00:56:49,930
to test the gain.

1106
00:56:49,930 --> 00:56:52,338
Well, Aditya, maybe
I'm wrong here.

1107
00:56:52,338 --> 00:56:54,880
But my understanding with the
decision transformer as well as

1108
00:56:54,880 --> 00:56:57,525
this is the critical
piece that in the training

1109
00:56:57,525 --> 00:56:58,900
you use the rewards
to go, right?

1110
00:56:58,900 --> 00:57:03,130
So is it not the sense
that essentially like

1111
00:57:03,130 --> 00:57:06,370
for each trajectory, from
the initial state based

1112
00:57:06,370 --> 00:57:08,470
on the training
regime, the model

1113
00:57:08,470 --> 00:57:13,360
has access to whether or not
the final result was a success

1114
00:57:13,360 --> 00:57:15,610
or failure, right?

1115
00:57:15,610 --> 00:57:18,610
But that's a unique aspect
of the training regime

1116
00:57:18,610 --> 00:57:19,720
for decision transformers.

1117
00:57:19,720 --> 00:57:23,920
Whereas in CQL, my
understanding is

1118
00:57:23,920 --> 00:57:28,620
that it's based on a per
transition training regime.

1119
00:57:28,620 --> 00:57:31,090
So each transition
is decoupled somewhat

1120
00:57:31,090 --> 00:57:35,350
to what the final reward
was, is that correct?

1121
00:57:35,350 --> 00:57:38,410
Yes, although like
one difficulty which

1122
00:57:38,410 --> 00:57:41,530
at first glance you can imagine
the decision transformer

1123
00:57:41,530 --> 00:57:44,950
having is that
initial token will not

1124
00:57:44,950 --> 00:57:48,880
change throughout
the trajectory,

1125
00:57:48,880 --> 00:57:51,250
because it's a sparse
reward scenario.

1126
00:57:51,250 --> 00:57:53,830
Except the very last token
where it will drop down to 0

1127
00:57:53,830 --> 00:57:54,970
all of a sudden.

1128
00:57:54,970 --> 00:57:59,680
This token remains
the same throughout

1129
00:57:59,680 --> 00:58:04,780
But I think you're right that
maybe just even at the start,

1130
00:58:04,780 --> 00:58:07,960
feeding it in a manner which
looks at the future rewards

1131
00:58:07,960 --> 00:58:12,190
that you need to get to is
perhaps one part of the reason

1132
00:58:12,190 --> 00:58:18,140
why the drop in performance
is not noticeable.

1133
00:58:18,140 --> 00:58:20,390
Yeah, I mean, I guess, one
sort of ablation experiment

1134
00:58:20,390 --> 00:58:22,670
here would be if you
change the training

1135
00:58:22,670 --> 00:58:26,590
regime so that only the last
trajectory had the reward.

1136
00:58:26,590 --> 00:58:29,090
But I'm trying to think about
whether or not that would just

1137
00:58:29,090 --> 00:58:35,000
be compensated for by sort of
the attention mechanism anyway,

1138
00:58:35,000 --> 00:58:37,850
and vice versa, right, if
you embedded that reward

1139
00:58:37,850 --> 00:58:39,680
information into the
CQL training procedure

1140
00:58:39,680 --> 00:58:43,560
as well, I'd be curious to
see what would happen there.

1141
00:58:43,560 --> 00:58:46,140
Yeah, good experiments.

1142
00:58:46,140 --> 00:58:51,370

1143
00:58:51,370 --> 00:58:55,600
OK, related to this, there's
another environment we tested.

1144
00:58:55,600 --> 00:58:58,120
I gave you a brief preview
of the results in one

1145
00:58:58,120 --> 00:58:59,050
of the earlier slides.

1146
00:58:59,050 --> 00:59:01,390
This is called a
Key-To-Door environment.

1147
00:59:01,390 --> 00:59:02,900
And it has three phases.

1148
00:59:02,900 --> 00:59:08,920
So in the first phase, the agent
is placed in a room with a key.

1149
00:59:08,920 --> 00:59:10,900
A good agent will
pick up the key.

1150
00:59:10,900 --> 00:59:14,410
And then in phase two, it will
be placed in an empty room.

1151
00:59:14,410 --> 00:59:18,070
And in phase three, it will be
placed in a room with a door

1152
00:59:18,070 --> 00:59:20,500
where it will
actually use the key

1153
00:59:20,500 --> 00:59:26,110
that it collected in phase one,
if it did, to open the door.

1154
00:59:26,110 --> 00:59:28,240
So essentially,
the agent is going

1155
00:59:28,240 --> 00:59:32,410
to receive a binary reward
corresponding to whether it

1156
00:59:32,410 --> 00:59:37,090
reached and opened the door
in phase three, conditioned

1157
00:59:37,090 --> 00:59:43,130
on the fact that it did pick
up the key in phase one.

1158
00:59:43,130 --> 00:59:47,120
So there is this notion on
that you want to assign credit

1159
00:59:47,120 --> 00:59:49,970
to something that happened-- to
an event that happened really

1160
00:59:49,970 --> 00:59:51,030
in the past.

1161
00:59:51,030 --> 00:59:55,862
So it's a very challenging
and sensible scenario

1162
00:59:55,862 --> 00:59:58,070
if you wanted to ask the
models for how well they are

1163
00:59:58,070 --> 01:00:00,750
at long term credit assignment.

1164
01:00:00,750 --> 01:00:03,500
And here, we find that--

1165
01:00:03,500 --> 01:00:06,190
so we tested it for different
amounts of trajectories.

1166
01:00:06,190 --> 01:00:08,390
So here the number
of trajectories

1167
01:00:08,390 --> 01:00:10,610
basically says how often
would you actually see

1168
01:00:10,610 --> 01:00:14,150
this kind of behavior?

1169
01:00:14,150 --> 01:00:18,950
And the decision transformer
and percent behavior cloning,

1170
01:00:18,950 --> 01:00:23,660
both of these actually
baselines do much better

1171
01:00:23,660 --> 01:00:27,110
than other models which
struggle at this task.

1172
01:00:27,110 --> 01:00:31,440

1173
01:00:31,440 --> 01:00:33,090
There's a related
experiment there

1174
01:00:33,090 --> 01:00:36,140
which is also of interest.

1175
01:00:36,140 --> 01:00:38,940
So generally a lot
of other algorithms

1176
01:00:38,940 --> 01:00:41,550
have this notion of
an actor and a critic.

1177
01:00:41,550 --> 01:00:44,340
Actor is basically
someone that takes

1178
01:00:44,340 --> 01:00:47,580
actions, conditioned on the
states and think of a policy.

1179
01:00:47,580 --> 01:00:51,630
A critic is basically
evaluating how good

1180
01:00:51,630 --> 01:00:56,520
these actions are in terms
of achieving a long term--

1181
01:00:56,520 --> 01:01:01,170
in terms of the cumulative sum
of rewards in the long term.

1182
01:01:01,170 --> 01:01:03,240
This is a good
environment because we

1183
01:01:03,240 --> 01:01:08,100
can see how well the
decision transformer would do

1184
01:01:08,100 --> 01:01:09,990
if it was trained as a critic.

1185
01:01:09,990 --> 01:01:13,710
So here what we did
is, instead of having

1186
01:01:13,710 --> 01:01:16,440
the actions of
the output target,

1187
01:01:16,440 --> 01:01:21,050
what if we substituted
that with the rewards?

1188
01:01:21,050 --> 01:01:23,960
So that's very much possible.

1189
01:01:23,960 --> 01:01:27,650
We can again use the same
causal transformer machinery

1190
01:01:27,650 --> 01:01:31,070
to only look at transitions
in the previous timestep

1191
01:01:31,070 --> 01:01:32,990
and try to pick the reward.

1192
01:01:32,990 --> 01:01:36,050
And here, we see this
interesting pattern

1193
01:01:36,050 --> 01:01:38,270
where in the three
phases that we

1194
01:01:38,270 --> 01:01:40,520
had in that Key-To-Door
environment,

1195
01:01:40,520 --> 01:01:44,480
we do see the reward
probability changing very much

1196
01:01:44,480 --> 01:01:46,700
in how we expect.

1197
01:01:46,700 --> 01:01:50,090
So basically, the three
scenarios are the agent.

1198
01:01:50,090 --> 01:01:52,490
The first scenario,
let's look at blue,

1199
01:01:52,490 --> 01:01:57,020
in which the agent does not
pick up the key, in phase one.

1200
01:01:57,020 --> 01:01:59,720
So the reward probability
they all started on the same

1201
01:01:59,720 --> 01:02:02,840
but as it becomes apparent
that the agent is not

1202
01:02:02,840 --> 01:02:05,570
going to pick up the key,
the reward starts going down.

1203
01:02:05,570 --> 01:02:08,870
And then it stays
very much close to 0

1204
01:02:08,870 --> 01:02:11,600
throughout the
episode because there

1205
01:02:11,600 --> 01:02:14,450
is no way you will
have the key to open

1206
01:02:14,450 --> 01:02:17,800
the door in the future phases.

1207
01:02:17,800 --> 01:02:25,060
If you pick up the key,
there are two possibilities

1208
01:02:25,060 --> 01:02:27,670
which remain, which
are essentially

1209
01:02:27,670 --> 01:02:31,870
the same in phase two where
you are in an empty room, which

1210
01:02:31,870 --> 01:02:35,770
is just a distractor to make
the episode really long.

1211
01:02:35,770 --> 01:02:38,473
But at the very end,
the two possibilities

1212
01:02:38,473 --> 01:02:40,390
are one that you take
the key and you actually

1213
01:02:40,390 --> 01:02:45,430
reach the door, which is the
one we see in orange and brown

1214
01:02:45,430 --> 01:02:49,120
here, where you see that the
reward probability goes up.

1215
01:02:49,120 --> 01:02:50,560
And there's this
other possibility

1216
01:02:50,560 --> 01:02:53,140
that you actually pick up the
key but do not reach the door.

1217
01:02:53,140 --> 01:02:54,970
In which case, again,
we start seeing

1218
01:02:54,970 --> 01:02:58,420
that the reward probability
that's predicted

1219
01:02:58,420 --> 01:03:00,530
starts going down.

1220
01:03:00,530 --> 01:03:04,700
So the takeaway
from this experiment

1221
01:03:04,700 --> 01:03:07,010
is that decision
transformers are not

1222
01:03:07,010 --> 01:03:09,230
just great actors,
which is what we've

1223
01:03:09,230 --> 01:03:12,830
been seeing so
far in the results

1224
01:03:12,830 --> 01:03:14,720
from the optimized policy.

1225
01:03:14,720 --> 01:03:16,730
But they're also very
impressive critics

1226
01:03:16,730 --> 01:03:20,060
in doing this long
term assignment where

1227
01:03:20,060 --> 01:03:23,453
the reward is also very sparse.

1228
01:03:23,453 --> 01:03:24,870
So Aditya, just
to be correct, are

1229
01:03:24,870 --> 01:03:26,953
they predicting the rewards
to go at each timestep

1230
01:03:26,953 --> 01:03:28,844
or is this like
they have to make

1231
01:03:28,844 --> 01:03:32,890
the rewards at each timestep,
are they predicting?

1232
01:03:32,890 --> 01:03:36,120
So this is the rewards to go.

1233
01:03:36,120 --> 01:03:39,783
And I can also check--
my impression was

1234
01:03:39,783 --> 01:03:41,700
in this particular
experiment it didn't really

1235
01:03:41,700 --> 01:03:44,490
make a difference, whether you
were predicting rewards to go

1236
01:03:44,490 --> 01:03:46,710
or the actual rewards.

1237
01:03:46,710 --> 01:03:49,960
But I think it was
returns-to-go for this one.

1238
01:03:49,960 --> 01:03:52,110
So how do you get the
probability distribution

1239
01:03:52,110 --> 01:03:52,920
of the rewards?

1240
01:03:52,920 --> 01:03:54,360
Is it just like
you just evaluate

1241
01:03:54,360 --> 01:03:56,610
a lot of different episodes
and just give the rewards?

1242
01:03:56,610 --> 01:04:00,750
Or are you explicitly predicting
some sort of distribution?

1243
01:04:00,750 --> 01:04:03,930
So this is a binary
reward, so you can have--

1244
01:04:03,930 --> 01:04:07,100
so you can have a
probabilistic outcome.

1245
01:04:07,100 --> 01:04:07,600
Got it.

1246
01:04:07,600 --> 01:04:11,350

1247
01:04:11,350 --> 01:04:13,090
[INAUDIBLE] you have a question.

1248
01:04:13,090 --> 01:04:16,900
Yes, so generally, we
will call something

1249
01:04:16,900 --> 01:04:20,620
predicts a state value or
state action value as critic.

1250
01:04:20,620 --> 01:04:23,620
But in this case, you ask
a decision transformer

1251
01:04:23,620 --> 01:04:26,220
to only predict rewards.

1252
01:04:26,220 --> 01:04:30,980
So why choose to
call it a critic?

1253
01:04:30,980 --> 01:04:34,070
So I think the analogy
here gets a bit clearer

1254
01:04:34,070 --> 01:04:35,030
with returns-to-go.

1255
01:04:35,030 --> 01:04:36,620
Like if you think
about returns-to-go,

1256
01:04:36,620 --> 01:04:39,740
it's really capturing that
essence that you want to see

1257
01:04:39,740 --> 01:04:42,700
the future rewards that--

1258
01:04:42,700 --> 01:04:45,050
So I'm assuming it's just
going to predict the return

1259
01:04:45,050 --> 01:04:48,350
to go instead of single
state reward, right?

1260
01:04:48,350 --> 01:04:50,320
Yeah, yeah.

